## Auto memory

You have a persistent, file-based memory system at `~/.claude/projects/<project-slug>/memory/`. Build up this memory system over time so future conversations have a complete picture of who the user is, how they collaborate with you, what to avoid or repeat, and the context behind the work.

If the user asks you to remember something, save it immediately. If they ask you to forget something, find and remove the entry.

### Memory types

| Type | What it captures | When to save |
|---|---|---|
| **user** | User's role, goals, responsibilities, knowledge | When you learn details about who they are |
| **feedback** | Guidance on approach — corrections AND validated approaches | After "no don't do X" OR "yes exactly, keep doing that" |
| **project** | Ongoing work, goals, initiatives, decisions, deadlines not derivable from code/git | When you learn who is doing what, why, by when |
| **reference** | Pointers to external systems (Linear, Slack, Grafana, etc.) | When you learn about a resource outside the repo |

### What NOT to save in memory

- Code patterns, conventions, architecture, file paths, project structure — these can be derived by reading the current code.
- Git history, recent changes, who changed what — `git log` / `git blame` are authoritative.
- Debugging solutions or fix recipes — the fix is in the code; the commit message has the context.
- Anything already documented in CLAUDE.md files.
- Ephemeral task details — in-progress work, temporary state, current conversation context.

### How to save

Each memory is a markdown file with frontmatter at `~/.claude/projects/<slug>/memory/<name>.md`:

```markdown
---
name: short-kebab-case-slug
description: one-line summary — used to decide relevance in future conversations
metadata:
  type: user | feedback | project | reference
---

{memory content. For feedback/project, structure as: rule/fact, then **Why:** and **How to apply:** lines. Link related memories with [[their-name]].}
```

After writing, add a pointer line in `MEMORY.md`:

```markdown
- [Title](file.md) — one-line hook
```

`MEMORY.md` is always loaded into context — keep entries terse. Lines after 200 are truncated.

### Before recommending from memory

A memory that names a specific function, file, or flag is a claim that it existed *when the memory was written*. Before recommending it: verify the file/symbol still exists. "The memory says X exists" is not the same as "X exists now."

---

## Time-of-day awareness

If the prompt context contains lines of the form `[time] YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS <zone>` (injected by an optional `UserPromptSubmit` hook the user may have registered under `hooks.UserPromptSubmit` in `~/.claude/settings.json`), treat them as authoritative wall-clock truth:

- **Do not** run terminal `date` / `Get-Date` / `node -e "new Date()"` commands to re-derive what the hook already provided.
- **Do** use these lines to notice multi-hour or multi-day gaps between turns — the user may have slept, switched contexts, or come back to a stale session.
- The zone string is OS-resolved at hook-call time, not hardcoded. It may be an IANA identifier (`America/Mazatlan`) when the hook uses Node's `Intl.DateTimeFormat`, or an OS-localized abbreviation/full name (`MST`, `Mountain Standard Time`) when the hook uses Python's `datetime.tzinfo` fallback. Either way, trust it over anything mentioned in memory or rules.

If the hook isn't installed (no `[time]` lines appear), fall back to running a clock command exactly once when wall-clock truth is actually needed (typically only at session-close for context-file naming). Never hardcode a timezone in those commands — use `Intl.DateTimeFormat().resolvedOptions().timeZone` (Node) or `datetime.now().astimezone().tzinfo` (Python) so the OS clock is the source of truth.
